<body data-view="both">

  <header class="topbar">
    <div class="topbar-inner">
      <div class="brand">
        <div class="logo">🌤️</div>
        <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue?
          <span>天空为什么是蓝色的？</span>
        </h1>
      </div>
      <div class="toggle" role="group" aria-label="Language view">
        <button data-view="both" aria-pressed="true">Both 双语</button>
        <button data-view="en" aria-pressed="false">English</button>
        <button data-view="cn" aria-pressed="false">中文</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="intro">
      <p>One explanation, three audiences — presented side by side in English and 中文.</p>
    </section>

    <nav class="nav" aria-label="Sections">
      <a href="#toddler">🍼 Toddler 幼儿</a>
      <a href="#teens">🎓 High School 高中</a>
      <a href="#phd">📚 Physics PhD 博士</a>
    </nav>

    <!-- ============ TODDLER ============ -->
    <article class="card" id="toddler">
      <div class="card-head">
        <div class="emo">🍼</div>
        <h2>For a 3-Year-Old Toddler
          <small>给 3 岁的幼儿</small>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="col col-en">
          <span class="lang">English</span>
          <p>The sky looks blue because the sun is a big, bright light — like a giant flashlight up in the sky. That light has lots of colors in it (like a rainbow!). When the light hits the air around us, the little blue pieces of light bounce around everywhere, so when you look up, your eyes see all that bouncy blue light. It's like when you shine a flashlight into foggy air and you can see the light going all around!</p>
          <p class="note">If you ever see the sky turn orange or red at sunset, it's because the sun is lower down, so the blue pieces have to bounce around <em>so many times</em> that they get tired and go away, and the red pieces get to show themselves! 🌅</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col col-cn">
          <span class="lang">中文</span>
          <p>天空看起来是蓝色的，因为太阳是一个又大又亮的光——就像天上的一支巨型手电筒。那束光里包含了很多颜色（就像彩虹一样！）。当光照到周围的空气时，蓝色的小光片到处弹跳，所以当你抬头看时，你的眼睛就看到了那些到处弹跳的蓝光。就像你把手电筒照进雾蒙蒙的空气里，能看到光到处散射一样！</p>
          <p class="note">如果你看到日落时天空变成橙色或红色，那是因为太阳位置变低了，蓝色的小光片必须弹跳<em>那么多次</em>以至于它们“累”了消失了，而红色的光片就能显现出来了！🌅</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- ============ HIGH SCHOOL ============ -->
    <article class="card" id="teens">
      <div class="card-head">
        <div class="emo">🎓</div>
        <h2>For a High School Teenager
          <small>给高中生</small>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="col col-en">
          <span class="lang">English</span>
          <p>The sky is blue due to a process called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>. Sunlight is actually white light, a mixture of all the colors (wavelengths) of visible light. When sunlight enters the atmosphere, it collides with the tiny gas molecules (mainly nitrogen and oxygen) that make up the air.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Scattering depends strongly on wavelength.</strong> Rayleigh scattering is inversely proportional to the fourth power of wavelength:
              <div class="formula">I ∝ 1 / λ⁴</div>
            </li>
            <li>Shorter wavelengths (blue/violet) are scattered roughly <strong>(500 / 250)⁴ ≈ 16× more</strong> than longer wavelengths (red).</li>
            <li>Violet (≈400 nm) is scattered even more than blue, but the sky looks blue because:
              <ol>
                <li>The Sun emits less violet light than blue light.</li>
                <li>Our eyes are more sensitive to blue than violet.</li>
                <li>Some violet light is absorbed by the upper atmosphere (ozone).</li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li>The scattered blue light is sent in all directions, so wherever you look, you see blue light coming toward you.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="bonus"><strong>Bonus — why sunsets are red/orange:</strong> When the Sun is near the horizon, sunlight travels through much more of the atmosphere. By then, most of the short-wavelength (blue/violet) light has been scattered out of the direct beam, leaving mostly the longer-wavelength reds and oranges to reach your eyes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col col-cn">
          <span class="lang">中文</span>
          <p>天空之所以是蓝色的，是由于一种叫做<strong>瑞利散射</strong>（Rayleigh scattering）的过程。阳光实际上是白光，它是所有可见光颜色（波长）的混合。当阳光进入大气层时，它会与构成空气的微小气体分子（主要是氮气和氧气）发生碰撞。</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>散射强烈依赖于波长。</strong>瑞利散射与波长的四次方成反比：
              <div class="formula">I ∝ 1 / λ⁴</div>
            </li>
            <li>较短的波长（蓝/紫）被散射的程度大约是较长波长（红）的 <strong>(500 / 250)⁴ ≈ 16 倍</strong>。</li>
            <li>紫色光（≈400 nm）比蓝光散射得更多，但天空看起来是蓝色的，因为：
              <ol>
                <li>太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少。</li>
                <li>我们的眼睛对蓝光的敏感度高于紫光。</li>
                <li>部分紫光被高层大气（臭氧）吸收。</li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li>散射后的蓝光向各个方向传播，所以无论你看天空的哪个方向，都会看到朝你而来的蓝光。</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="bonus"><strong>附加知识——为什么日落是红色/橙色：</strong>当太阳靠近地平线时，阳光要穿过更厚的大气层。到那时，大部分短波长（蓝/紫）光已经被散射出直射光束，只剩下较长波长的红色和橙色光到达你的眼睛。</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- ============ PHD ============ -->
    <article class="card" id="phd">
      <div class="card-head">
        <div class="emo">📚</div>
        <h2>For a Physics PhD
          <small>给物理学博士</small>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="col col-en">
          <span class="lang">English</span>

          <p>The blue color arises from <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong> of solar radiation by atmospheric constituents whose characteristic size <em>a</em> is much smaller than the wavelength <em>λ</em> of incident light (the Rayleigh regime, <em>a</em> ≪ <em>λ</em>, or equivalently size parameter <em>x</em> = 2π<em>a</em>/<em>λ</em> ≪ 1).</p>

          <h3>Scattering Formalism</h3>
          <p>The differential cross-section for elastic scattering from a dielectric sphere in the Rayleigh limit is:</p>
          <div class="formula">dσ/dΩ = (9π/4) · [(m²−1)² / (m²+2)²] · (r⁶/λ⁴) · (1 + cos²θ)</div>
          <p>where <em>m</em> is the refractive index relative to the medium, <em>r</em> its radius, <em>λ</em> the vacuum wavelength, and <em>θ</em> the scattering angle. The total cross-section scales as σ ∝ λ⁻⁴. In a molecular gas the relevant quantity is the <strong>polarizability</strong> α; for unpolarized light the per-molecule cross-section is:</p>
          <div class="formula">σ = (8π/3) · k⁴ · α² · (1 − cos²θ) / …  (integrated form)</div>
          <p>with angular dependence governed by the <strong>depolarization factor</strong> p. For N₂ and O₂ at optical wavelengths, p ≈ 0.022 (small but nonzero due to the anisotropic polarizability of N₂).</p>

          <h3>Why Not Violet?</h3>
          <p>Although the cross-section peaks toward violet (~400 nm) over red (~700 nm) by (700/400)⁴ ≈ 4.4, the observed blue results from the interplay of:</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Solar spectral radiance</strong> — the Sun's ~5778 K blackbody spectrum decreases in flux toward violet, and photospheric absorption shapes the incident spectrum.</li>
            <li><strong>Aerial transmission</strong> — the column-integrated optical depth τ(λ) grows rapidly toward the UV; by ~300 nm ozone (Hartley &amp; Huggins bands) and O₂ (Chappuis) absorb strongly, and even at 400 nm the single-scattering albedo and multiple-scattering statistics favor blue in the diffuse radiance.</li>
            <li><strong>Human visual sensitivity</strong> — the CIE photopic response V(λ) is significantly higher at 450 nm (blue) than 400 nm (violet), and the S-cone response is weaker, suppressing violet.</li>
          </ol>

          <h3>Multiple Scattering &amp; Radiative Transfer</h3>
          <p>In a plane-parallel atmosphere with optical depth τ = σnH (<em>n</em> = number density, <em>H</em> = scale height), the sky radiance solves the <strong>RTE</strong>:</p>
          <div class="formula">dI(λ,μ,φ)/dτ = −I + (ω/4π) ∫₄π P(Ω′·Ω) I′ dΩ′ + S(λ,μ,φ)</div>
          <p>where ω is the single-scattering albedo, P the phase function (Rayleigh: P ∝ 1 + cos²θ), and S the source function (solar irradiance modified by the direct-beam transmittance e^(−τ/|μ₀|)). Where τ(λ) ~ 1 in the blue, diffuse radiance is substantial; in red, τ ≪ 1, so the sky is dominated by single-scattering of the attenuated beam and is much dimmer.</p>

          <h3>Higher-Order Considerations</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Depolarization</strong> — Rayleigh scattering is partially polarizing; at 90° from the Sun, polarization degree P = 1/(1+p). Historically this is how the Rayleigh regime was confirmed.</li>
            <li><strong>Mie &amp; aerosols</strong> — larger particles (aerosols, droplets, dust) scatter at x ~ 1 to x ≫ 1 per Mie theory: strongly forward-peaked, weaker wavelength dependence (∝ λ^(−α), α &lt; 4, often ~0–2 for the coarse mode). This is why hazy skies look whitish/grayish, and why the <strong>Raman effect</strong> (inelastic) is negligible for color yet measurable spectroscopically.</li>
            <li><strong>Refraction &amp; the dark zenith</strong> — near the horizon, refraction and extinction (esp. aerosols) shift apparent position and color; the top of the sky can look darker blue due to a longer path with more cumulative scattering and absorption.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Summary</h3>
          <p>The blue is the emergent property of: (a) a λ⁻⁴ cross-section favoring short wavelengths, (b) the incident solar spectrum, (c) atmospheric absorption and optical-depth filtering, and (d) human spectral sensitivity. None alone fully explains the hue — the color is a <strong>joint spectral-integration problem</strong> across the radiation field, atmospheric optics, and the observer.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="col col-cn">
          <span class="lang">中文</span>

          <p>天空的蓝色源于太阳辐射被大气成分产生的<strong>瑞利散射</strong>，这些成分的特征尺寸 <em>a</em> 远小于入射光波长 <em>λ</em>（瑞利区，<em>a</em> ≪ <em>λ</em>，等价于尺寸参数 <em>x</em> = 2π<em>a</em>/<em>λ</em> ≪ 1）。</p>

          <h3>散射形式</h3>
          <p>在瑞利极限下，电介质球体弹性散射的微分截面为：</p>
          <div class="formula">dσ/dΩ = (9π/4) · [(m²−1)² / (m²+2)²] · (r⁶/λ⁴) · (1 + cos²θ)</div>
          <p>其中 <em>m</em> 为散射体相对介质的折射率，<em>r</em> 为半径，<em>λ</em> 为真空波长，<em>θ</em> 为散射角。总截面按 σ ∝ λ⁻⁴ 缩放。在分子气体中，相关量为分子的<strong>极化率</strong> α；对非偏振光，每个分子的散射截面为：</p>
          <div class="formula">σ = (8π/3) · k⁴ · α² · (1 − cos²θ) / …  （积分形式）</div>
          <p>其角度依赖由<strong>去极化因子</strong> p 决定。氮气和氧气在光学波长下 p ≈ 0.022（很小但不为零，源于 N₂ 的各向异性极化率）。</p>

          <h3>为什么不是紫色？</h3>
          <p>尽管截面在紫光端（~400 nm）相对红光（~700 nm）的峰值约为 (700/400)⁴ ≈ 4.4 倍，但观察到的蓝色是以下因素相互作用的结果：</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>太阳谱辐射亮度</strong>——太阳 ~5778 K 黑体光谱向紫光方向流量减小，光球层吸收塑造了入射光谱。</li>
            <li><strong>大气透过率</strong>——沿光程积分的光学厚度 τ(λ) 向紫外方向迅速增大；到 ~300 nm 时臭氧（Hartley 与 Huggins 带）和 O₂（Chappuis 带）强烈吸收，即使在 400 nm 处，单次散射反照率与多次散射统计也使漫射辐射中蓝色胜过紫色。</li>
            <li><strong>人眼视觉敏感度</strong>——CIE 明视觉响应 V(λ) 在 450 nm（蓝）处显著高于 400 nm（紫），且 S 锥细胞响应较弱，从而抑制紫色。</li>
          </ol>

          <h3>多次散射与辐射传输</h3>
          <p>在具有光学厚度 τ = σnH（<em>n</em> 为数密度，<em>H</em> 为标高）的平面平行大气中，天空的辐射亮度是<strong>辐射传输方程（RTE）</strong>的解：</p>
          <div class="formula">dI(λ,μ,φ)/dτ = −I + (ω/4π) ∫₄π P(Ω′·Ω) I′ dΩ′ + S(λ,μ,φ)</div>
          <p>其中 ω 为单次散射反照率，P 为相位函数（瑞利：P ∝ 1 + cos²θ），S 为源函数（经直射束透过率 e^(−τ/|μ₀|) 修正的太阳辐照度）。在蓝色波段 τ(λ) ~ 1 时，漫射辐射亮度相当可观；在红光处 τ ≪ 1，天空辐射由被衰减光束的单次散射主导，且暗淡得多。</p>

          <h3>高阶考量</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>去极化</strong>——瑞利散射具有部分偏振特性；在与太阳成 90° 的方向，偏振度 P = 1/(1+p)。历史上正是由此确认了瑞利区。</li>
            <li><strong>米氏（Mie）散射与气溶胶</strong>——更大的粒子（气溶胶、水滴、尘埃）按尺寸参数 x ~ 1 到 x ≫ 1 依米氏理论散射：强烈前向峰化，波长依赖变弱（∝ λ^(−α)，α &lt; 4，粗模常在 ~0–2）。这就是朦胧天空呈白色/灰色而非深蓝色的原因，也解释了<strong>拉曼效应</strong>（非弹性）对颜色可忽略却可被光谱测量。</li>
            <li><strong>折射与“黑色”天顶</strong>——在地平线附近，折射与消光（尤其气溶胶）会改变视位置与颜色；天空最顶部因更长路径累积更多散射与吸收而可能呈现更深的蓝色。</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>总结</h3>
          <p>蓝色是以下因素共同作用的新兴性质：(a) 偏好短波长的 λ⁻⁴ 散射截面，(b) 入射太阳光谱，(c) 大气吸收与光学厚度过滤，以及 (d) 人类光谱敏感度。其中任何一个单独都无法完全解释观察到的色调——颜色是辐射场、大气光学性质与观察者之间的<strong>联合光谱积分问题</strong>。</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <footer>
      Why Is the Sky Blue? · 天空为什么是蓝色的？ &nbsp;—&nbsp; bilingual, single-page, no external dependencies.
    </footer>
  </main>

